{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>His firstborn ox, majesty is his.</b> There are instances of בכור that are an expression of greatness and kingship, as in 'also I shall make him firstborn' (Psalms 89:28), and likewise 'My son, My firstborn, Israel' (Exodus 4:22). The king who emerges from him is Joshua. His strength is fierce like an ox to conquer many kings, and <b>his horns are the horns of a wild ox</b>: an ox's strength is fierce but its horns are not beautiful; a wild ox's horns are beautiful but its strength is not fierce. To Joshua He gave the strength of an ox and the beauty of a wild ox's horns. <b>With them he shall gore the myriads of Ephraim</b> — those who are gored are the myriads whom Joshua, who came from Ephraim, killed. <b>And the thousands of Manasseh</b> — the thousands that Gideon killed of Midian, as it says (Judges 8:10), 'Now Zebah and Zalmunna were in Karkor,' and several thousands were counted there. This is the language of Rashi.\n\nNow, the verse is very garbled according to this interpretation, for <b>his horns are the horns of a wild ox</b> is a designation for the firstborn ox, who is Joshua, and if so, how would he gore with them the thousands of Manasseh, who are those killed by Gideon?\n\nThe language of the Sifre is: since Scripture did not specify for us how many Joshua killed of the Canaanites, it teaches through 'Now Zebah and Zalmunna were in Karkor' — all together 135,000; therefore it says 'and they are the thousands of Manasseh.' And their intent in this is to expound that also <b>with them he shall gore together the ends of the earth</b> alludes to Joshua, and it says that these ends of the earth that he shall gore are the myriads that Ephraim — that is, Joshua — killed, and they are the thousands that Manasseh killed afterward; for Joshua killed all of them, and their children who arose after them — Manasseh killed some thousands of them. And so they taught there: 'With them he shall gore peoples together' — now what did Joshua conquer? Did he not conquer only thirty-one kings? This teaches that he conquered kings and rulers from one end of the world to the other. 'Together the ends of the earth' — now what lands did Joshua conquer? Did he not conquer only this small portion? Rather, all the kings he subjugated were kings and rulers.\n\nThe plain sense of the verse is that because in blessing Joseph with produce he likened him to an ox, for 'much produce is in the strength of an ox' (Proverbs 14:4), and he said that the firstborn that he would beget would have the majesty of kingship, alluding to Joshua, who is the first of the house of Joseph, and he would have greatness and kingship. <b>And his horns are the horns of a wild ox</b> — the horns of the aforementioned ox — <b>and with them he shall gore together the ends of the earth</b>. And he said that his horns, which are two, are the myriads of Ephraim and the thousands of Manasseh: the right horn for Ephraim and the left for Manasseh. Now in his blessing he mentioned that they would be myriads and thousands, in the sense of 'the myriads of thousands of Israel' (Numbers 10:36)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes the Sifre midrash which reads the verse as Joshua's conquests spanning the world; the word פרטום (partum) in the Sifre quotation appears to be a transcription of a foreign land-name or region, rendered here as 'portion' (חלק) per the parenthetical gloss in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:59:38.794787812Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}